Native O2 and Octane bring-up
===========================

This is the native platform work underlying the O2 and Octane milestone in
../../../xiri/docs/ROADMAP.md.  It is separate from IRIX ABI compatibility.
The integration branch is xiri in both NetBSD and IRIS.  Existing qualified
Indy kernels, detached lab worktrees, IRIX 6.5.22 and 6.5.30 images stay pinned
until their replacements pass their own qualification.

O2 / IP32
---------

NetBSD already has CRIME/MACE support in GENERIC64_IP3x.  The retained
XIRI_O2 kernel in the sibling lab reaches the framebuffer, serial ports,
Ethernet and PCI.  The remaining recorded SCSI-stage RI trap was reproduced
without disks on 2026-11-15 and traced to overwritten exception vectors.

The vectors are intact at ahc_pci_attach entry.  Its controller register
accesses use the wide MACE PCI memory window at physical 0x280000000.
IRIS 7d46498b truncates this to 0x80000000, then applies the no-ECC RAM
alias.  Register writes corrupt the TLB refill handler; the later memset
store takes a TLB miss and runs that damaged handler.  This is distinct
from the already fixed NetBSD R10000 cache-alignment bug.

The IRIS correction keeps the wide PCI I/O and memory windows separate
from RAM and preserves their byte lanes.  The unchanged XIRI_O2 kernel now attaches both SCSI controllers, reads an
8-GB CHD, mounts its FFS root read-only and runs native single-user commands.
The candidate halted cleanly to the PROM, with original images unchanged.
The 14 PCI tests and 213 CPU executor tests pass.  This establishes basic
storage and a native shell, not sustained DMA/write integrity, networking,
graphics or an IRIX desktop.  Those remain the next O2 qualification stages.

Octane / IP30
-------------

GENERIC64_IP3x currently supports O2; its name does not imply Octane support.
Octane needs a separate board port: ARCS64 entry and firmware services,
HEART memory/interrupts/timer, XIO/Crossbow and Bridge bus/DMA support,
IOC3 serial/RTC/Ethernet/input, and PCI storage.  Start with one CPU and
serial boot.  IMPACT/VPro graphics and SMP follow basic native operation.

ip30/arcs64-probe.c is a standalone N64 firmware diagnostic, not a kernel.
It demonstrates ELF64 loading at 0xa800000020080000 (physical 0x20080000),
ARCS64 console output, 64-bit argv/environment access, memory-descriptor
iteration and return to the firmware.  It retains the PROM stack and
exception handlers and does not initialize NetBSD or claim any drivers.
The load address is a tested diagnostic placement, not a final kernel ABI.
A kernel port also needs to audit KSEG0 assumptions and the current
-msym32 flags in sys/arch/mips/conf/Makefile.mips; changing TEXTADDR alone
cannot make the existing kernel run at an XKPHYS address.

The 2026-11-15 diskless IRIS run passed with one R10000 and 512 MB.  The
firmware described the diagnostic's page as LoadedProgram, enumerated
0x20000 4-KB pages, printed XIRI_IP30_ARCS64_OK and returned to the PROM.
Physical hardware has not been tested.

M1 kernel entry (2026-11-15): a dedicated XIRI_IP30 kernel now reaches its
real entry at cached XKPHYS 0xa800000020010000, clears BSS, switches to a
kernel-owned stack, validates the ARCS64 SPB, prints entry/CP0/firmware and
memory-descriptor evidence, prints XIRI_IP30_KERNEL_ENTRY_OK and stops
deliberately.  The kernel is built without -msym32 and without an ELF32
wrapper; Octane RAM is outside the KSEG0/KSEG1 direct map.  This is an entry
checkpoint, not a bootstrap: exceptions, pmap, interrupts and devices are
M2/M3 work.  The rebuilt O2 kernel still reaches native single-user and
clean halt, and the rebuilt XIRI_INDY64 kernel still boots the Indy disk to
login and clean halt.  The design is ip30/DESIGN and the retained evidence
and hashes are ip30/2026-11-15-m1-entry.json.

M2a memory/vector bootstrap (2026-11-15): XIRI_IP30_M2 continues past the
M1 proof, reconciles the CCA 5 entry alias with NetBSD's cached XKPHYS/TLB
aliases, installs the low exception vectors, initialises ARCS64 memory
descriptors and reaches pmap_bootstrap() plus the lwp0 uarea.  It stops at
XIRI_IP30_M2_MEMORY_OK before autoconfiguration and before enabling HEART
interrupts or a timer.  The checkpoint reports 512 MB (65534 8-KiB pages)
and four memory clusters with the kernel LoadedProgram region excluded.
The low-memory cpu_info and slow-fault cells are initialized before vector
installation.  XIRI_IP30_M2_TLB proves a successful TLB refill (counter
0 -> 1); XIRI_IP30_M2_SLOWFAULT verifies the original EPC, BadVAddr and
cause in mips3_slowfault before UVM handling, emits
XIRI_IP30_M2_SLOWFAULT_OK, and halts.
Evidence is in ip30/2026-11-15-m2a-memory.json; the M2 design notes are in
ip30/DESIGN.

M2b HEART/clock (2026-11-15): XIRI_IP30_M2B continues into
autoconfiguration, attaches mainbus/cpu0/heart0, masks and clears HEART,
passes a mask/pending/acknowledge self-test and prints
XIRI_IP30_M2B_HEART_OK.  An IP30 IPL map masks HEART IP2..IP6 at IPL_VM
and above; HEART handlers are accepted at IPL_VM only.  CP0 Count/Compare
supplies hardclock and the timecounter; a five-tick callout prints
XIRI_IP30_M2B_CLOCK_OK and then a vector-0 HEART handler proves masked
(deferred) and unmasked delivery, printing XIRI_IP30_M2B_INTR_OK.  The boot
then reaches an unconfigured `root device:` prompt.  IOC3 serial is still
polled through ARCS64.  Evidence is in ip30/2026-11-15-m2b-heart-clock.json.

The implementation handover is ip30/HANDOVER.  Work continues on
topic/sgimips-octane, progressing from the M1/M2a/M2b checkpoints to serial
multi-user login.  It includes the source audit, retained evidence,
build/boot commands, milestones and regression requirements.
Tracking issue: https://github.com/salvogendut/NetBSD-xiri/issues/1

Reproduce the Octane diagnostic from the NetBSD checkout:

    sh xiri/platforms/ip30/build-probe.sh \
      ../xiri/.work/netbsd64-build/tools/bin/mips64--netbsd- \
      /tmp/xiri-ip30-build

    python3 xiri/platforms/probe.py \
      --xiri ../xiri \
      --emulator ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/native-20261115/emulator \
      --machine octane --image /tmp/xiri-ip30-build/arcs64-probe \
      --nvram ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/octane-20261114/inputs/nvram.bin \
      --flash ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/octane-20261114/inputs/flash.bin \
      --expect XIRI_IP30_ARCS64_OK --return-to-prom \
      --output /tmp/xiri-ip30-probe

Use a fresh output directory for each run.  The harness requires the
sibling Xiri lab and an emulator directory containing iris and iris-ci.
It records input hashes, the executed script and command, serial output,
registers, cleanup and unchanged originals.  It copies mutable firmware
state and attaches no disks unless --disk is supplied.  That option makes
a private reflink/copy of a stopped CHD and enables an overlay beside it;
never supply an actively running disk.  Firmware SCSI diagnostic warnings are
expected for this diskless fixture.  Local control sockets must be allowed.

Build and boot the M1 checkpoint from the NetBSD checkout.  Prepare isolated
tools first; the Toolbox is needed if the host has no C++ compiler:

    octane_build="$PWD/../xiri/.work/netbsd-octane-build"
    ./build.sh -m sgimips -a mips64eb -U -u -j 2 \
      -O "$octane_build/obj" -T "$octane_build/tools" \
      -D "$octane_build/dest" -V MKX11=no \
      -V 'HOST_CC=cc -std=gnu17' -V 'HOST_CFLAGS=-O2 -std=gnu17' \
      kernel.gdb="$PWD/sys/arch/sgimips/conf/XIRI_IP30"

    python3 xiri/platforms/probe.py \
      --xiri ../xiri \
      --emulator ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/native-20261115/emulator \
      --machine octane \
      --image "$octane_build/obj/sys/arch/sgimips/compile/XIRI_IP30/netbsd" \
      --nvram ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/octane-20261114/inputs/nvram.bin \
      --flash ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/octane-20261114/inputs/flash.bin \
      --expect XIRI_IP30_KERNEL_ENTRY_OK --kernel-entry \
      --output ../xiri/.work/octane-entry-YYYYMMDD

The kernel ELF entry and its single PT_LOAD segment are both
0xa800000020010000 (physical 0x20010000).  There must be no netbsd.elf32 for
this configuration.  The runner stops the emulator after the marker and
records registers, CP0, the SPB page and the first 128 bytes of kernel text.
It fails the run unless the printed entry equals the image ELF e_entry, the
stack pointer lies inside the reserved boot stack, and the BSS witness is
zero.  The deliberate halt is a nop/branch spin loop; a monitor-induced
Debug Break at that stop is expected and is not a kernel fault.

Build and run the M2a memory/vector checkpoint with the M2 configuration:

    ./build.sh -m sgimips -a mips64eb -U -u -j 2 \
      -O "$octane_build/obj" -T "$octane_build/tools" \
      -D "$octane_build/dest" -V MKX11=no \
      -V 'HOST_CC=cc -std=gnu17' -V 'HOST_CFLAGS=-O2 -std=gnu17' \
      kernel.gdb="$PWD/sys/arch/sgimips/conf/XIRI_IP30_M2"

    python3 xiri/platforms/probe.py \
      --xiri ../xiri \
      --emulator ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/native-20261115/emulator \
      --machine octane \
      --image "$octane_build/obj/sys/arch/sgimips/compile/XIRI_IP30_M2/netbsd" \
      --nvram ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/octane-20261114/inputs/nvram.bin \
      --flash ../xiri/.work/platform-checks/octane-20261114/inputs/flash.bin \
      --expect XIRI_IP30_M2_MEMORY_OK --kernel-entry \
      --output ../xiri/.work/octane-m2a-YYYYMMDD

The M2a kernel deliberately halts after printing the memory accounting.
Its timestamps read [1.0000000] because no clock or timer is attached yet.
XIRI_IP30_M2_TLB exercises a successful TLB refill and stops on
XIRI_IP30_M2_TLB_REFILL_OK.  XIRI_IP30_M2_SLOWFAULT prints
XIRI_IP30_M2_SLOWFAULT_BEGIN, verifies the original EPC/BadVAddr/cause in
mips3_slowfault and stops on XIRI_IP30_M2_SLOWFAULT_OK; it does not enter
the generic UVM path.

For O2 use --machine o2, omit --flash and --return-to-prom, and select the
ELF32 PROM wrapper (the underlying kernel is N64), for example
../xiri/.work/netbsd-o2-build/tftp/netbsd-XIRI_O2.  Choose --expect to name
an actual milestone printed by that kernel, such as "root device" for a
diskless boot.  To check native userland, add --disk with a stopped NetBSD
CHD, --single-user and --expect "Enter pathname of shell".  This enters
the shell, runs uname and mount, and requires a clean halt back to the PROM.
Use a disk-backed output directory for CHD tests.  A panic, exception, timeout,
missing marker or failed cleanup is a failed run, never a boot success.

References
----------

- ../xiri/lab/irix-o2/README.md and its retained kernel/build manifest.
- ../xiri/docs/results/2026-11-14-octane-platform.md, the earlier loading
  failures with the O2 kernel's ELF32 wrapper and incompatible RAM layout.
- sys/dev/arcbios/: the existing 32-bit firmware ABI, including when called
  by a 64-bit NetBSD kernel; it is not the Octane ARCS64 ABI.
- Linux's public ARCS interface descriptions, consulted for interface
  widths and function numbers (no implementation copied):
  https://kernel.googlesource.com/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux/+/refs/heads/master/arch/mips/include/asm/sgiarcs.h
  https://kernel.googlesource.com/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux/+/refs/heads/master/arch/mips/include/asm/fw/arc/types.h
- MACE specification section 9.3, pp. 159-160, for the PCI address map;
  retained locally in ../iris/docs/datasheets/o2/mace.pdf.
